mpleted","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":6,"requested":{"background":2,"foreground":4,"unset":0},"started_in_background":2,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":6,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"material":6}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":116,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Cycle 130 is closed, and it produced no new VI. The P3b-1 trial build in LabVIEW stopped at step 26 of 31, so the real build was not run.\n\n**What worked:**\n- **Memory check:** it now predicts each build step's peak memory from its plan. It fails any step over 675 MB, or any step it cannot predict. Its self-test passes 9/0.\n- **P3b re-cut:** the step is split by memory into P3b-1 (31 edits, 663.4 MB predicted) and P3b-2 (39 edits, 664.3 MB predicted). Together they reach the same final diagram as the unsplit 70 edits.\n- **Dry-run check:** it now fails when the simulated run stops before the last edit. Until now it passed a run that stopped at 48 of 63.\n- **Tooling backlog:** four of the queued wrongly refused commands are cleared, and the stop record no longer treats `md5sum`/`grep` of a build script as a launch.\n\n**What failed:**\n- **Trial build:** edits 1–25 matched the simulator exactly. At edit 26 LabVIEW named a new Flat Sequence tunnel `Image Out`, where the simulator predicted no name. This is the second run in a row stopped by a tunnel name. The simulator's naming rule was inferred from one case and is now wrong on a second, so no third run until it is measured. Memory was 650.4 MB at that point, under the prediction.\n- **After the stop:** closing the panel hung for about 7 minutes and LabVIEW had to be killed. The work VI is unchanged.\n- **Lost time:** two of the six helper sessions went on releasing review holds. One hold was a wrong refusal by the review gate (`guard_peer`); the other was a missing reviewer on my tooling card. The retrospective counted the gate's wrong refusal as a failed safeguard; I accepted that and recorded a fix for cycle 131.\n\n**Next cycle starts with** an offline card that measures the tunnel-naming rule from every tunnel-creating edit recorded so far, then fixes the simulator and re-checks P3b-1. Next to it runs the `guard_peer` fix. Then comes the P3b-1 trial build and one real build. The decisions are PD268–270 in `docs/d1/tooling.md` and `docs/d1/ring-p3b.md`. The next act is in `tools/bench/next.json` and STATUS `## NEXT`.\n\nOne user decision is still open: D-2026-10-02-01, a check that stops a helper agent from finishing while its own LabVIEW run is live.\n\nSeveral MCP connectors (Figma, Amplitude, Atlassian, BigQuery, Hex) need authorizing in your connector settings, and the Definite server failed to connect. None of them were needed for this cycle.","ttft_ms":2086,"type":"result","duration_ms":6051334,"uuid":"bf58254a-f048-41b8-9f45-c4181974d3d9","ttft_stream_ms":1739,"time_to_request_ms":246,"first_content_frame_ms":1739,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
